     - Look: two panels of one width each, black at 50%, the game's own font (`HudPipeline::push_text`) at the `scaled_gs` scale the menus use, so it follows the GUI Scale option (3 on a 1600 x 720 screen: 8-px glyphs are 24 px, rows 30 px; the whole screen takes 10 rows on the left and 8 on the right). A grey label column, then the values, so a value is always in the same place. Colours carry meaning only where it helps: FPS green from 50, yellow from 25, red below; biome green; the aimed block cyan; LoD yellow.
     - Left panel (the player): XYZ (feet, 2 decimals), block, chunk and the cell inside it, facing (compass + bearing + pitch, up is +), biome (not in the Nether), light (sky / block raw at the eye cell), time of day (tick 0 = 06:00) with rain / thunder, and the block aimed at with its cell. Right panel (the engine): FPS now and average, CPU ms and the worst frame of the last second (the numbers of the `FPS:` log line), surface size and the scene scale in percent (dynamic resolution), renderer, render distance in chunks, chunks loaded / meshed, LoD on / off (+ the LoD debug mode when one is on: its title is part of the panel while this screen is on, not drawn at the top centre), world. Render distance and LoD state are rows of their own so a screenshot at 16 chunks with the LoD off says so.
